IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `nfp-dates`
IMPORT `nfp-rules`

-- Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) -- the family member's questions.
--
-- SEAT: the family member of a person who has died, left out of the will or left too little by it or by the intestacy
-- rules, who claims (or may claim) provision out of the estate: the spouse or de facto partner, a former spouse or de
-- facto partner, a child, stepchild or grandchild, or a parent. Every question is asked as that person would ask it, and
-- takes plain facts that person could state; the Court's judgments are asked as facts ("Is the Court satisfied that
-- ...?"). The rules are in nfp-rules.l4; this module builds their inputs and restates none. The time limit (s 9) is a
-- DEONTIC rule run inside `Is my application in time`; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Not an official version of the Family Provision Act 1970 (NT). Copyright in Northern Territory legislation
-- is owned by the Northern Territory. Quoted under the Northern Territory copyright policy of 8 October 1996; see
-- SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Family Provision Act 1970 (NT): the family member's questions`

§§ `Who may claim`

@export Am I entitled to apply to the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory for provision out of the estate of a person who has died (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 7)? A spouse or de facto partner and a child always may; a former spouse or de facto partner and a stepchild only if the deceased was maintaining them; a grandchild only if their parent (the deceased's child) died first, or a living parent was not maintaining them; a parent only if the deceased was maintaining them or left no spouse, partner or child. Anyone else is not listed in the Act.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc How you were related to the person who died: their spouse or de facto partner at the death; a former spouse or former de facto partner; their child (including one born alive after the death); their stepchild; their grandchild; their parent; or another relationship, or none
      `was there a maintenance order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before the death, a court order was in force requiring the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit
      `was the deceased maintaining me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before the death, the deceased was maintaining you, under a written agreement or otherwise
      `was the deceased contributing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before the death, the deceased was making a contribution to your maintenance
      `was it more than nominal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that contribution, in all the circumstances, can be regarded as more than a nominal one
      `could a court have ordered maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court would, if the deceased were still living, have power to order the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit
      `did my parent die first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a grandchild: whether your parent who was the deceased's child died before the deceased
      `was a parent of mine alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a grandchild: whether one or both of your parents were alive when the deceased died
      `was a living parent maintaining me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a grandchild: whether that living parent (or either of them) was maintaining you immediately before the deceased died
      `did the deceased leave a spouse or partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a parent: whether the deceased was survived by a spouse or de facto partner
      `did the deceased leave a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a parent: whether the deceased was survived by any of their children
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to apply` MEANS
    `s 7 -- I am entitled to apply` (`The claimant` WITH
        `relationship`                                    IS `my relationship`
        `maintained by the deceased`                      IS `s 7(7) -- I was maintained by the deceased immediately before the death` `was there a maintenance order` `was the deceased maintaining me` `was the deceased contributing` `was it more than nominal` `could a court have ordered maintenance`
        `my parent who was the deceased's child died before the deceased` IS `did my parent die first`
        `a parent of mine was alive at the death`         IS `was a parent of mine alive`
        `a living parent maintained me before the death`  IS `was a living parent maintaining me`
        `the deceased left a spouse or de facto partner`  IS `did the deceased leave a spouse or partner`
        `the deceased left a child`                       IS `did the deceased leave a child`)

§§ `When I must apply`

DECLARE `The last day` IS ONE OF
    `the 12 months have not begun: no administration has been granted in the Territory`
    `the last day to apply is` HAS `the day` IS A DATE

@export By what day must I apply for provision out of the estate (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 9(1))? Within 12 months after the grant of probate or letters of administration in the Northern Territory (or an order to collect to the Public Trustee, or the sealing here of a grant from elsewhere). The Court can extend the time.
GIVEN `has administration been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate of the will or letters of administration have been granted in the Northern Territory, or an order to collect and administer the estate granted to the Public Trustee or Curator of Deceased Estates, or a grant from elsewhere sealed by the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory
      `the grant date` IS A DATE @desc The date of that grant, order or sealing
GIVETH A `The last day`
`By what day must I apply` MEANS
    IF `has administration been granted`
    THEN `the last day to apply is` (`s 9(1) -- the last day to apply` `the grant date`)
    ELSE `the 12 months have not begun: no administration has been granted in the Territory`

@export Is my application for provision out of the estate in time (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 9)? It is made on the day the document starting it is filed in the Supreme Court, and must be within 12 months after the grant of administration unless the Court has extended the time. If it is late you may ask the Court to extend the time, unless the estate has already been lawfully and fully distributed.
GIVEN `has administration been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate, letters of administration, an order to collect to the Public Trustee or Curator, or the sealing of a grant from elsewhere has been made in the Northern Territory
      `the grant date` IS A DATE @desc The date of that grant, order or sealing
      `the filing date` IS A DATE @desc The day you filed (or will file) the notice of motion or other document starting your application in the Supreme Court
      `has the Court extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has extended the time for your application
      `the extended last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time the Court extended to (any date if it has not)
      `has the estate been fully distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the whole estate has already been lawfully and fully distributed
GIVETH A `Timing`
`Is my application in time` MEANS
    `s 9 -- whether my application is in time` (`The application` WITH
        `administration granted`        IS `has administration been granted`
        `grant day`                     IS `the grant date`
        `filing day`                    IS `the filing date`
        `time extended by the Court`    IS `has the Court extended the time`
        `extended last day`             IS `the extended last day`
        `estate fully distributed`      IS `has the estate been fully distributed`)

-- A situation: only once you have applied.
@export Must I serve notice of my application on the administrator of the estate (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 10(1))? Once you apply, you must have notice served on each executor or administrator. The Court may order notice served on others too.
GIVEN `have I applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made an application for provision out of the estate
      `is there an administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anyone has been granted probate or letters of administration of the estate in the Northern Territory
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I serve notice of my application on the administrator` MEANS
    IF `have I applied`
    THEN IF `is there an administrator` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `What the Court may order for me`

@export Can the Supreme Court order that provision be made for me out of the estate (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) ss 7, 8, 9, 22)? Only if you are entitled to apply, your application is in time (or the time was extended), and the Court is satisfied that the will or the intestacy rules do not leave you adequate provision for your proper maintenance, education and advancement in life. The Court then has a discretion, considers the deceased's reasons, and may refuse because of your character or conduct.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc How you were related to the person who died: their spouse or de facto partner at the death; a former spouse or former de facto partner; their child (including one born alive after the death); their stepchild; their grandchild; their parent; or another relationship, or none
      `was the deceased maintaining me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before the death, the deceased was maintaining you or making a more than nominal contribution to your maintenance, a maintenance order was in force against the deceased for you, or a court could have ordered the deceased to pay you maintenance
      `did my parent die first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a grandchild: whether your parent who was the deceased's child died before the deceased
      `was a parent of mine alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a grandchild: whether one or both of your parents were alive when the deceased died
      `was a living parent maintaining me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a grandchild: whether that living parent (or either of them) was maintaining you immediately before the deceased died
      `did the deceased leave a spouse or partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a parent: whether the deceased was survived by a spouse or de facto partner
      `did the deceased leave a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a parent: whether the deceased was survived by any of their children
      `is my application in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application was filed within 12 months after the grant of administration, or within time the Court extended
      `is the Court satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that adequate provision is not available to you from the estate, under the will or the intestacy rules (counting benefits given by will through a power of appointment), for your proper maintenance, education and advancement in life
      `does my conduct disentitle me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Court's opinion, your character or conduct disentitles you to an order
GIVETH AN `Outcome`
`Can the Court order provision for me` MEANS
    `s 8 -- what the Court may do`
        (`s 7 -- I am entitled to apply` (`The claimant` WITH
            `relationship`                                    IS `my relationship`
            `maintained by the deceased`                      IS `was the deceased maintaining me`
            `my parent who was the deceased's child died before the deceased` IS `did my parent die first`
            `a parent of mine was alive at the death`         IS `was a parent of mine alive`
            `a living parent maintained me before the death`  IS `was a living parent maintaining me`
            `the deceased left a spouse or de facto partner`  IS `did the deceased leave a spouse or partner`
            `the deceased left a child`                       IS `did the deceased leave a child`))
        `is my application in time`
        `is the Court satisfied`
        `does my conduct disentitle me`

@export Can provision for me come out of property the deceased appointed by their will under a power of appointment (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 13)? Only for a power under which the deceased could have appointed the property to themselves, and only if the Court is satisfied that other estate property cannot justly provide for you or that special circumstances call for it.
GIVEN `have I applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for provision out of the estate
      `did the will exercise a power of appointment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased, by their will, exercised a general or special power of appointment over the property
      `could the deceased have appointed it to themselves` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before death, that power entitled the deceased to appoint the property to themselves
      `is the Court satisfied other property cannot justly provide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that adequate provision for you cannot justly be made out of other property of the estate (including property a general gift in the will carries under s 35 of the Wills Act 2000)
      `is the Court satisfied special circumstances exist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that, because of special circumstances, provision should be made out of or charged on that property
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Can provision for me come out of property appointed by the will` MEANS
    `s 13(1) -- the Court may order provision out of the appointed property` `have I applied` `did the will exercise a power of appointment` `could the deceased have appointed it to themselves` `is the Court satisfied other property cannot justly provide` `is the Court satisfied special circumstances exist`

§§ `Property already distributed`

-- A situation: only where the administrator has already distributed some of the estate.
@export Can the Court order provision for me out of estate property the administrator has already distributed (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 20)? Only if your application was made within 12 months after the grant and the property was distributed before the administrator had notice of it, and not if the order would disturb a proper distribution made for someone who depended on the deceased.
GIVEN `the grant date` IS A DATE @desc The date administration of the estate was granted in the Northern Territory
      `the filing date` IS A DATE @desc The day you filed the document starting your application
      `was it distributed before notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the administrator distributed the property before having notice of your application
      `was it a proper distribution for a dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was a proper one made to provide for the maintenance, education or advancement in life of a person who was totally or partially dependent on the deceased immediately before the death
GIVETH A `Distributed property answer`
`Can provision come out of property already distributed` MEANS
    `s 20 -- provision out of distributed property` (`the application was made within 12 months after the grant` `the grant date` `the filing date`) `was it distributed before notice` `was it a proper distribution for a dependant`

DECLARE `Action answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: section 21 bars that action against the administrator`
    `section 21 does not bar it: whether the action lies is for the general law`

-- A situation: only where the administrator has already distributed some of the estate.
@export Can I sue the administrator for having distributed the estate (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 21)? Not if the distribution was a proper one for a dependant of the deceased, or if it was made before the administrator had notice of your application (or of an application to extend time) after the administrator had advertised for claims under s 96 of the Administration and Probate Act 1969 and the time for claims had passed.
GIVEN `was it a proper distribution for a dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was a proper one made to provide for the maintenance, education or advancement in life of a person totally or partially dependent on the deceased immediately before the death
      `was it distributed before notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the administrator distributed before having notice of your application, or of an application to extend the time
      `were claims advertised and closed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before distributing, the administrator had given notices under section 96 of the Administration and Probate Act 1969 and the time they set for sending in claims had expired
GIVETH AN `Action answer`
`Can I sue the administrator for distributing` MEANS
    IF `s 21 -- no action lies against the administrator for the distribution` `was it a proper distribution for a dependant` `was it distributed before notice` `were claims advertised and closed`
    THEN `no: section 21 bars that action against the administrator`
    ELSE `section 21 does not bar it: whether the action lies is for the general law`

§§ `Once an order is made for me`

-- A situation: only once the Court has ordered provision for you.
@export How does an order for provision for me take effect (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 16)? As if it were a codicil to the will made just before the death, or, for property passing on intestacy, as a change to the intestacy rules.
GIVEN `the will and the estate` IS A `The estate` @desc Whether a will disposes of the whole estate, there is no will, or a will disposes of only part of the estate
GIVETH AN `Effect of the order`
`How does the order for me take effect` MEANS
    `s 16 -- how the order takes effect` `the will and the estate`

-- A situation: only once the Court has ordered provision for you.
@export Can the Court discharge, vary, suspend or increase the order made for me (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 17)? On an application by the administrator or a person interested in the estate it may discharge, vary or suspend the order, but not increase it under s 17(1). Where it ordered periodical payments or an invested lump sum and is satisfied you have otherwise come to have the means, it may also make such other order as is just. You must be served with notice of the application.
GIVEN `who is applying` IS A `Who applies` @desc Who is applying to change the order: the administrator; a person beneficially entitled to or interested in part of the estate; or someone else
      `what change` IS A `Change sought` @desc Whether the application seeks to discharge, reduce or suspend the order, or to increase the provision
      `is it periodical or an invested lump sum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court ordered periodical payments for you, or a lump sum to be invested for your benefit
      `is the Court satisfied I have other means` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that you have otherwise come to have, or become entitled to, means for your proper maintenance, education or advancement in life
GIVETH A `Variation answer`
`Can the Court change the order made for me` MEANS
    `s 17 -- what the Court may do to the order` `who is applying` `what change` `is it periodical or an invested lump sum` `is the Court satisfied I have other means`

-- A situation: only once provision has been or is to be ordered for you.
@export Will a mortgage, charge or assignment of the provision ordered (or to be ordered) for me have any effect (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 19)? Only if the Court gives permission.
GIVEN `does the Court permit it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has given permission for the mortgage, charge or assignment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Will a mortgage or assignment of my provision have effect` MEANS `does the Court permit it`

-- A situation: only where administration was granted on a presumption that the deceased had died.
@export If administration was granted because the deceased was presumed dead, and the grant is revoked because they were alive, what must I give back (Family Provision Act 1970 (NT) s 14)? Only if the Court directed an undertaking or security: restore property received under the order (or at your option pay its value when received), and pay back money received.
GIVEN `did the Court direct an undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court directed that your provision be made only on your undertaking or security under section 14
      `was the grant revoked because they were alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the grant of administration has been revoked on the ground that the person was living at the time of the grant
      `money received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of money you received under the order (0 if none)
      `value of property received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollar value, at the time you received it, of property other than money you received under the order (0 if none)
GIVETH A `What I must give back`
`What must I give back if the presumed dead person is alive` MEANS
    `s 14 -- what my undertaking requires` `did the Court direct an undertaking` `was the grant revoked because they were alive` `money received` `value of property received`
